A||N||3||Two88GTs||AC Rapidfire questions||||1||
Z||000000||Two88GTs||08-01-2001||09:37 PM||||Saw two different versions of AC RapidFire #1s on the shelf at Wally World today.  One type had a "ribbed" ceramic section with a jagged center electrode and the other set had a smooth ceramic section and a smooth center electrode.  Which is the newer version and does anyone have a preference over one or the other?  I'm attempting to change the plugs on my 61k GT; the front 3 plugs came off with ease although the ceramic broke on two of them and the rear plug closest to the airbox came off pretty easily too even though it was rusty like you wouldn't believe.  The other two I'm having a bit of trouble(probably rust buildup).||||reg||1
Z||000001||kslish||08-01-2001||11:36 PM||||I was in Wal-mart a few days ago getting some Rapidfires for my Formula and noticed the same thing.<P>The ones that have the smooth ceramic and smooth center electrode are platinum tipped.  I believe the package says Rapidfire Platinum somewhere on the package, but it isn't that noticible.  What surprised me most was the fact that they Wal-Mart is charging the same price for both the plain Rapidfires and the Platinums.  The UPC codes on the packages are completely different, but Wal-mart doesn't seem to differentiate the two....placing them in the isles with both types intermixed and the same price at the register.<P>I ended up searching through the aisle and buying a set of the older style steel splined tip Rapidfires since I have no experience with the newer Rapidfire Platinums and haven't heard anything about them yet.<P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rodmcneill||08-01-2001||11:46 PM||||There was a change in the rapidfire plugs. The older original plugs have the ribbed insulator and a bigger star shaped center electrode. I was surprised that they eliminated the ribs on the body of the plug. I had always heard this was supposed to help eliminate flashover(the spark traveling over the outside of the plug instead of through the center electrode) especially with todays hot coils and ignition systems. The new rapidfires seem to work fine. How long that tiny new center electrode lasts...||||reg||1
Z||000003||ginoferrari||08-02-2001||12:19 AM||||Does the 2.5 use the same plug as the 2.8? I noticed at wallmart that it lists a Rapidfire for the 2.8 but not the 2.5. But it lists the same AC Delco resistor plug for both.||||reg||1
